<resources>
    <!-- Enable auxiliary cameras selector. -->
    <bool name="config_enableAuxCameras">true</bool>

    <!--
         The array has to stay declared even while it is empty: Aperture reads it
         out of the overlay package by name at startup, and with the overlay
         applied but the name gone the lookup returns id 0 and
         ApertureApplication dies before it can draw anything
         (Resources$NotFoundException in OverlaysRepository.getStringArray).
         Learned the hard way 2026-08-07 - removing it stopped the camera from
         opening at all.

         It is empty on purpose.

         The cameras do advertise 60 fps behind MediaTek's own streaming mode -
         com.mediatek.streamingfeature.availableHfpsMaxResolutions reads
         1920x1080@60 on every camera and 3840x2160@60 on the main and the
         telephoto, EIS included - and asking for it does reach the HAL. What it
         does not do is come out of the recorder: with hfpsMode=1 both as a
         request key and as a session parameter, and the frame rate picker on 60,
         the file still measures 30.008 fps.

         The missing half is the encoder. CameraX takes the recording frame rate
         from the CamcorderProfile, and media_profiles_V1_0.xml - which is
         byte-identical to stock - only declares 1080p and 2160p at 30. Listing
         60 here without that would put a frame rate in the UI that the file
         never has, so it stays out until the profile side is done.

         2160p60 additionally took camerahalserver down hard enough to kill the
         app, and media_codecs_performance measures the HEVC encoder at 13-29 fps
         on 3840x2160, so that one is not worth chasing at all.
    -->
    <string-array name="config_additionalVideoConfigurations" />
</resources>
